posts.body:
For what it's worth, my
Battler Flipbooks Core
does animate with loose frame images rather than spritesheets. It's also quite slim at runtime, despite the amount of text in the file. Most of that is related to setting up the animations by mouse in the plugin parameters.
(I've since updated it to be officially cross-version, though I think it actually just worked in MZ too from the get-go. Yay for doing only one thing (and careful use of transparent hooks)
)
Note that it's a plugin framework that needs to be instantiated by a "Layer" to actually do anything.
I do charge for the ready-made ones, but it's an open system (I guarantee API stability for all my plugins) and there's an example layer under CC0 here:
Battler_Flipbooks_LAYER.js#L758-L774 (GitHub/Tamschi)
The example layer has everything that's needed in terms of boilerplate and parameter definitions, but only triggers each time the battler takes ("performs", it's subtly different) damage. The link should highlight the part of the file that needs to be adjusted. In your case, I'd replace it with the following code (taken from my
Battler Entrance Flipbooks
):
JavaScript:
...core.makeHook(() => api, BattleManager, function setup() {
    const result = api.oldSetup.apply(this, arguments);
    for (const battler of BattleManager.allBattleMembers()) {
        runtime.applyRules(battler, {});
    }
    return result;
}),
By setting a very high repeat limit, the looping section of the "Flipbook" effectively becomes an idle animation.
